## The Swing: who is watching whom?
feedback:
    style: dry
    perfect: Fragonard would trust you with a secret.
    good: You found the lover. The husband never does.
    poor: The husband would sympathise.
    none: Like the husband, you just kept pushing.
sources:
    - id: wallace
      title: Les hasards heureux de l'escarpolette (The Swing)
      authors: The Wallace Collection, London
      year: 2024
      finding: c. 1767–68, 81 × 64.2 cm; the woman between her elderly husband and her young lover, the flying slipper and the offered hat
      url: https://www.wallacecollection.org/art/collection/collection-highlights/les-hazards-heureux-de-lescarpolette-swing/
    - id: gac
      title: The Swing
      authors: The Wallace Collection on Google Arts & Culture
      year: 2024
      finding: 'Collé''s account: a gentleman of the court asked Doyen for his mistress on a swing pushed by a bishop, himself watching her legs; Doyen refused and named Fragonard; the older man is no longer a priest; the statue follows Falconet''s L''Amour menaçant'
      url: https://artsandculture.google.com/asset/the-swing/HgHhMSaHgUZPSQ
    - id: huntington
      title: Cupid (L'Amour menaçant), after Falconet
      authors: The Huntington
      year: 2024
      finding: Falconet's seated Cupid, finger to his lips; the marble, commissioned by Madame de Pompadour, shown at the Salon of 1757
      url: https://emuseum.huntington.org/objects/12754/cupid

    card title "The Swing"

This is Fragonard's The Swing, from about 1767.
    image art cites ["wallace"] credit "Fragonard, c. 1767–68. Wallace Collection, London. Public domain." x 295 y 340 height 640 url https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/eb/Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg/1920px-Fragonard%2C_The_Swing.jpg look screen regions.cupid [0,0.455,0.12,0.19] regions.cupid_hush [0.045,0.45,0.07,0.09] regions.dog [0.725,0.88,0.1,0.08] regions.garden [0,0.44,0.95,0.5] regions.hat [0.265,0.718,0.06,0.045] regions.husband [0.715,0.7,0.18,0.21] regions.husband_face [0.83,0.7,0.06,0.05] regions.lover [0.065,0.77,0.26,0.13] regions.lover_face [0.085,0.77,0.07,0.06] regions.putti [0.525,0.715,0.16,0.12] regions.scene [0.05,0.48,0.88,0.44] regions.skirts [0.3,0.6,0.32,0.15] regions.slipper [0.205,0.515,0.05,0.05] regions.woman [0.26,0.49,0.44,0.29] regions.woman_back [0.6,0.55,0.1,0.12] regions.woman_face [0.51,0.5,0.07,0.06]

A young woman in pink is flying high in a garden.
    highlight art:woman strength soft

And our ques (@highlight art:husband_face art:lover_face art:woman_face@)tion: who here can see what?

It was painted by Jean-Honoré Fragonard for a private client, on a canvas only about eighty centimetres tall.
    portrait fragonard x 760 y 380 width 190 of "Jean-Honoré Fragonard" cameo true

    erase fragonard

At the centre, she sits at the top of her arc, skirts billowing.
    highlight art:woman lift true

Behind her, half in the shade, an older man works the ropes. The museum calls him her husband.
    highlight art:husband

    ask "Where is the second man? Tap him." spot lover on art
        intro: Someone else is in this garden, and he is not supposed to be there.
        right: There he is, half hidden in the roses.
        wrong: Look low down, on the left, in the bushes.

Down in the bushes lies a young man, gazing straight up into her flying skirts.
    highlight art:lover effect detail

At the top of the swing, one of her slippers flies off, in his direction.
    highlight art:slipper effect detail

He holds up his hat to her, as if to catch it, and he is plainly enjoying the view.
    highlight art:hat effect detail

    camera reset true

The French title calls it the happy accidents of the swing. Few viewers have believed in the accident.
    highlight art:slipper strength strong

    quiz "Who cannot see the young man? Pick one." on_canvas true buttons_at.x 780 buttons_at.y 380 buttons_layout column
        intro: So, who in the garden cannot see the young man?
        right: The older man: he stands behind her, in the shade, and the bushes hide the lover from him.
        wrong: Follow each one's eyes, and what stands in the way.
        + The older man
        * The woman
        * Us, the viewers

He smiles, pulls the ropes, and sees only (@highlight art:husband_face art:woman_back effect detail@) the back of her dress. He is, in effect, helping.

Only the little white dog at his feet barks, and nobody listens.
    highlight art:dog effect detail

Above the lover stands a stone Cupid with a finger to his lips, after a famous marble Falconet made for Madame de Pompadour.
    highlight art:cupid effect detail

    camera reset true

A writer of the time, Charles Collé, recorded the order: a gentleman of the court wanted his mistress on a swing, pushed by a bishop, with himself placed where he could see her legs.
    highlight art:woman art:husband art:lover strength soft

The painter Gabriel-François Doyen turned the job down; Fragonard took it, and made the  (@highlight art:husband@)bishop an ordinary older man.

So the man in the bushes plays the client's own part: he paid to watch, and he is in the picture, watching.
    highlight art:lover strength strong rest grey

Now count the views. The husband sees only her back.
    highlight art:husband_face art:woman_back

The lover looks up under her skirts, and she looks down at him.
    highlight art:lover_face art:skirts art:woman_face art:lover

Only we see all of it: the lover, the husband, and the secret between them.
    highlight art:scene strength strong lift true

That is why some read the Cupid's hush as aimed out of the frame, at the one person who knows everything: you.
    highlight art:cupid_hush effect detail

    camera reset true

So this is Fragonard's trick: Las Meninas put us where the king stood; here we are let in on a secret, and knowing it makes us part of the joke.
    highlight art:garden lift true

    quiz "A film director wants the audience to feel part of an affair. Which shot follows Fragonard's method?"
        right: We see the lover and the spouse doesn't: knowing more than someone in the scene makes the secret ours too.
        wrong: Ask who knows the most in each shot.
        + We see the lover hiding; the chatting spouse can't
        * We watch it all through the unaware spouse's eyes
        * The lovers kiss alone, with no one else near
        * The spouse finds them, and the secret is out

So keep the secret. The Cupid is still asking you to.
    highlight art:cupid_hush lift true

